course: chinese-street
lesson: 23
title: Friendly teasing — when it's fun and when it stings
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Lín trips over the football and his mate grins "bèndàn!" — "BUN-DAHN", literally "stupid egg" — and Lín grins back "nǐ cái shì bèndàn!", you're the dummy!: that is teasing between friends, and both of them are laughing.
explain: Mandarin teasing names are often funny pictures: shǎguā is a "silly melon", lǎnchóng a "lazy worm", dǎnxiǎoguǐ a "small-gall ghost" (scaredy-cat), xiǎoqìguǐ a stingy "ghost", àikūguǐ a "loves-crying ghost".
explain: The same word can be a cuddle or a punch: who says it, to whom, and with what face decides; shǎguā from Mum to a toddler is almost a hug, xiǎo pàngzi from a granny to a baby is praise, but from one kid to another about their body it hurts.
explain: Some teasing is never fair: calling a crying friend a crybaby, a poor friend a meanie, or someone who reports real danger a tattletale.
explain: "xiào sǐ wǒ le" — "laughing me to death" — is the Chinese LOL; but laughing at a friend who is really hurt is not funny in any language.
fact: "bèndàn" means silly or dummy, literally "stupid egg"; the cheeky answer is "nǐ cái shì bèndàn!", and it is rude to a struggling younger child or to an adult.
fact: "shǎguā" means silly-billy, literally "silly melon"; said with a smile, by parents to small kids or friends to friends, it is almost cuddly, while "shǎ" alone can be harsher.
fact: "dǎnxiǎoguǐ" means scaredy-cat, literally "small-gall ghost"; the answer is "wǒ cái bú pà!", but daring someone with it can push them into something dangerous.
fact: "xiǎoqìguǐ" means meanie, someone stingy who won't share; it is unfair if their family has little money.
fact: "lǎnchóng" means lazybones, literally "lazy worm", and parents use it fondly on Sunday mornings.
fact: "àikūguǐ" means crybaby, literally "loves-crying ghost"; it stings, because a friend who cries needs help, not a name.
fact: "chòuměi" means vain, a show-off about looks; chòu (smelly) appears in many teasing words meaning "too pleased with yourself".
fact: "gàozhuàngjīng" means tattletale; telling a grown-up about real danger or bullying is right, whatever anyone calls you.
fact: "xiǎo pàngzi" means chubby, "little fatty"; from a granny to a toddler it is praise, but from a kid to a kid it hurts, because body teasing is not friendly.
fact: "xiǎopéngyǒu" means little kid, literally "little friend"; from a teacher it is sweet, but from a big kid "nǐ shì xiǎopéngyǒu!" means "you're too little to play".
fact: "xiǎo pìhái" means brat or little pest; adults say it about naughty children and it is a bit rude.
fact: "xiào sǐ wǒ le" means "that's hilarious", literally "laughing me to death", after a joke or a funny video.
exercise: Lín misses an open goal and his mate laughs "bèndàn!". What does it mean? | Dummy, silly | Champion | Goalkeeper | Liar
exercise: What is bèndàn word for word? | Stupid egg | Silly melon | Lazy little worm | Small ghost
exercise: Lín's mate calls him bèndàn with a grin. What's the cheeky reply? | nǐ cái shì bèndàn! | xiào sǐ wǒ le! | wǒ cái bú pà! | xièxie!
exercise: Lín's mum ruffles his little sister's hair: "xiǎo shǎguā". How does it feel? | Almost cuddly, a fond tease | A harsh insult said to hurt | A telling-off for being silly | A scary threat
exercise: Lín won't jump off the low wall and his cousin says "dǎnxiǎoguǐ!". What is he called? | A scaredy-cat | A little crybaby | A lazybones who naps | A big show-off
exercise: Someone calls you dǎnxiǎoguǐ. What do kids shout back? | wǒ cái bú pà! | nǐ cái shì lǎnchóng! | xiào sǐ wǒ le! | bié kū!
exercise: Lín won't share his crisps: "xiǎoqìguǐ!". What is he called? | A meanie who won't share | A crybaby who wants his mum | A tattletale | A little kid who can't open the bag
exercise: Lín's dad calls him "lǎnchóng" at 11 on a Sunday, still in bed. What does it mean? | Lazybones, fondly | Scaredy-cat, angrily | Show-off, very rudely | Little brat, coldly
exercise: What is àikūguǐ? | Crybaby, "loves-crying ghost" | Scaredy-cat, "small-gall ghost" | Meanie, "stingy ghost" | Tattletale, "telling spirit"
exercise: Lín's sister checks her hair in every window. Lín laughs "chòuměi!". What does he mean? | Vain, a show-off about looks | Smelly, she badly needs a bath | Pretty, a real compliment | Shy, hiding her face from people
exercise: What does chòu (smelly) mean in teasing words like chòuměi? | Too pleased with yourself | Really smelly, like old socks | Very pretty indeed | Very hungry
exercise: A boy keeps running to the teacher over tiny things. Kids call him "gàozhuàngjīng". What is it? | Tattletale | Scaredy-cat | Big show-off | Little lazybones
exercise: Lín's granny pinches a baby's cheek: "xiǎo pàngzi!". What is she doing? | Praising a healthy baby | Insulting the baby's chubby cheeks | Telling the baby off | Asking the baby's name
exercise: The teacher says "xiǎopéngyǒumen, zǎo!". What did she call the class? | Little friends, kids | Silly little melons | Brats | Big boys and big girls
exercise: A big kid sneers "nǐ shì xiǎopéngyǒu!" at Lín. What does he mean? | You're a baby, too little to play | You're my little friend, come and play | You're very clever | You're very tall for your age
exercise: A grown-up mutters "xiǎo pìhái" about a boy kicking seats on the bus. What does it mean? | Brat, little pest | Little hero, brave one | Little friend of mine | Little genius, clever clogs
exercise: Lín watches a cat fall off a sofa on video: "xiào sǐ wǒ le!". What is he saying? | That's hilarious! | That's terrible! | I'm scared! | I'm bored!
test: Lín's mate calls a kid in Year 2 who can't read yet "bèndàn" in front of everyone. Friendly teasing? | No, to a struggling younger kid it's rude | Yes, bèndàn is always friendly, whoever hears it | Yes, it means "little friend" | No, it means a scaredy-cat
test: they're all on the roof of the shed going "dǎnxiǎoguǐ, jump!!". what should Lín do? | Not jump; the name isn't worth a broken arm | Jump to prove he isn't scared of anything | Push someone else off first | Call them àikūguǐ and jump
test: Lín calls a friend xiǎoqìguǐ for not buying sweets, but the friend's family has very little money. Is it fair? | No, that teasing is unfair and hurts | Yes, he really didn't share, so it's true | Yes, it's just a joke word, no harm | No, because xiǎoqìguǐ means "rich"
test: Lín's mate trips and is really crying with a cut knee. Someone shouts "àikūguǐ!". What's the right move? | Help him; a crier needs help, not a name | Laugh and join in the name-calling, it's only a joke | Walk away and play | Shout "xiào sǐ wǒ le!"
test: A girl tells the teacher a big kid is hitting her brother, and gets called gàozhuàngjīng. Was she wrong? | No, telling about bullying is right | Yes, never tell a teacher anything | Yes, she should hit back | No, but she must say sorry to him
test: Lín's mate calls another boy "xiǎo pàngzi" every day at lunch. The boy goes quiet. Friendly or not? | Not friendly: body teasing hurts | Friendly, it's what grannies say | Friendly, it means "healthy" | Not friendly, because it means "stupid"
test: Same word, same day: Lín's granny says "xiǎo pàngzi" to a baby and Lín's mate says it to a classmate. Why does one sting? | Who says it to whom decides; kid to kid about bodies hurts | One of them got the tone wrong, so it meant something rude | Babies don't understand Chinese | Grannies are allowed to be rude
test: In a cartoon a dad calls his son "xiǎo shǎguā" and hugs him. The subtitles say "you idiot!". What's wrong with the subtitle? | It's fond, more like "you silly-billy" | Nothing, it's a harsh insult, hug or not | It should say "you liar" | It should say "you little brat"
test: Lín falls off his bike, laughs and gets up fine, and you say "xiào sǐ wǒ le". OK or not? | OK if he's fine and laughing too | Never OK, it means "die" | OK even if he's badly hurt and crying | Not OK, it's a swear word
test: Lín's mum says "lǎnchóng, qǐ chuáng le!" (get up!). How cross is she? | Fond and teasing, not really cross | Furious, he's grounded for a week | Scared for him | Bored of him and his lie-ins
test: Which teasing name is "silly melon"? | shǎguā | bèndàn | lǎnchóng | chòuměi
test: Lín's mate reckons chòuměi means "smelly but pretty", so it's a compliment. Right? | No, it teases someone vain about looks | Yes, it's a real compliment, a nice one | Yes, it means smelly feet | No, it means a crybaby at school
exam: Identify the literal sense of the teasing term "bèndàn". | Stupid egg | Silly melon | Lazy, sleepy worm | Small-gall ghost
exam: It is given that one term of mild teasing is applied by a grandmother to an infant and by a pupil to a classmate concerning his physique. Select the statement consistent with the established principles of teasing. | Speaker and addressee determine whether it is fond or hurtful | The term is equally affectionate in both instances | The term is equally offensive in both instances | Only the tone of the vowels determines the effect of the term
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the reporting of genuine danger or bullying to an adult, notwithstanding the epithet "gàozhuàngjīng"? | It is the correct course of action | It constitutes a breach of loyalty to be avoided | It is permissible only on weekdays | It warrants an apology to the bully
exam: Ascertain the meaning of the expression "xiào sǐ wǒ le". | That is exceedingly amusing, literally "laughing me to death" | I am gravely ill and must lie down at once | I am about to depart | That is deeply saddening
